## 11. Ablation Analysis: What Actually Contributed?

| Configuration | Accuracy | Macro-F1 | Empirical Finding |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| **1. Tabular Only (Model A)** | 66.91% | 73.91% | Strong on ICMP/BULK; struggles severely on WEB vs INTERACTIVE boundary (INTERACTIVE F1 = 38.46%). |
| **2. Sequence Only (Model B1)** | 69.96% | 77.66% | Fine-grained temporal order improves discrimination, but volatile on unseen capture environments (Fold 5). |
| **3. Sequence + SSL (Model B3)** | 72.25% | 76.18% | Masked reconstruction pretraining within train fold regularizes sequence representations, boosting accuracy by **+2.29%** over B1. |
| **4. Sequence + Protocol Context (Model B2)** | 67.23% | 70.95% | Context markers (`is_esp`, `direction_conf`) are near-uniform across native-IPsec traffic, adding redundant parameters without discriminative variance. |
| **5. Tabular + Sequence Hybrid (Model C)** | **80.25%** | **81.89%** | **Optimal Configuration (+13.35% over XGBoost)**. Combines aggregate distribution stability with temporal sequence dynamics, solving the INTERACTIVE F1 bottleneck (63.46% vs 38.46%). |
| **6. Multi-View Full Fusion (Model D)** | 61.33% | 70.28% | Over-parameterized context view diluted gradient flow into tabular/sequence branches, causing underfitting under strict GroupKFold. |

